<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="Math234Sp25-Midterm2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Math234Sp25-Midterm2</title>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Let <me>f(x,y) = e^{2x+3y} + \frac{ x^2y^3}{12} +6.</me>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the tangent plane to <m>f</m> at <m>(3,-2).</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						f_x \amp = 2e^{2x+3y} + \frac{2xy^3}{12} \implies f_x(3,-2) = 2e^0 + \frac{6(-8)}{12} = 2 - 4 = -2 \\
						f_y \amp = 3e^{2x+3y} + \frac{3x^2y^2}{12} \implies f_y(3,-2) = 3e^0 + \frac{3(9)(4)}{12} = 3 + 9 = 12 \\
						f(3,-2) \amp = e^0 + \frac{9(-8)}{12} + 6 = 1 - 6 + 6 = 1
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					The equation of the tangent plane is:
					<me>z = -2(x-3) + 12(y+2) + 1.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use the tangent plane to <m>f</m> at <m>(3,-2)</m> to approximate the value of <m>f(2.9, -2.1).</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						f(2.9, -2.1) \amp \approx -2(2.9-3) + 12(-2.1+2) + 1 \\
						\amp = -2(-0.1) + 12(-0.1) + 1 \\
						\amp = 0.2 - 1.2 + 1 = 0.
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Let <m>z(x,y)</m> be defined implicitly by <me>x^2 z^2 + 2yz^3 = x^2e^y +2z</me> Compute <m>\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial x}.</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Let <m>F(x,y,z) = x^2z^2 + 2yz^3 - x^2e^y - 2z = 0</m>.
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\frac{\partial z}{\partial x} \amp = -\frac{F_x}{F_z} \\
					F_x \amp = 2xz^2 - 2xe^y \\
					F_z \amp = 2x^2z + 6yz^2 - 2 \\
					\frac{\partial z}{\partial x} \amp = -\frac{2xz^2 - 2xe^y}{2x^2z + 6yz^2 - 2}
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<task workspace="1.5in">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose <m>z=f( s, t)</m> where <m>s</m> and <m>t</m> are functions of <m>x,</m> <m>y</m> and <m>u</m>. Write an expression for  <m>\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial u}</m> using the Chain Rule.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>\frac{\partial z}{\partial u} = \frac{\partial z}{\partial s} \frac{\partial s}{\partial u} + \frac{\partial z}{\partial t} \frac{\partial t}{\partial u}.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>z = f(s, t)</m>, where <m>s = x+2y+3u</m>, <m>t = 4xy^2u^3</m>. Suppose <m>\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial s} =
											3s^2+2t,</m> and <m>\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial t} = 2t+2s.</m> Compute <m>\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial u}</m> when <m>x= 1,</m> <m>y = -1</m> and <m>u=2</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					First compute <m>s</m> and <m>t</m> at <m>(1,-1,2)</m>:
					<me>s = 1 + 2(-1) + 3(2) = 5, \quad t = 4(1)(-1)^2(2^3) = 32.</me>
					Compute the partial derivatives of <m>s</m> and <m>t</m> with respect to <m>u</m>:
					<me>\frac{\partial s}{\partial u} = 3, \quad \frac{\partial t}{\partial u} = 12xy^2u^2 \implies \frac{\partial t}{\partial u}(1,-1,2) = 12(1)(1)(4) = 48.</me>
					Now use the chain rule:
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\frac{\partial z}{\partial u} \amp = (3s^2 + 2t) \frac{\partial s}{\partial u} + (2t + 2s) \frac{\partial t}{\partial u} \\
						\amp = (3(25) + 2(32))(3) + (2(32) + 2(5))(48) \\
						\amp = (75 + 64)(3) + (64 + 10)(48) \\
						\amp = (139)(3) + (74)(48)
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Let <m>f(x,y) = x^2y + \cos( \pi xy)</m>. Find the directional derivative of the function <m>f(x,y)</m> at the point <m>(1,2)</m> in the direction of the point <m>(4,-3).</m> That is, find the directional derivative at <m>(1,2)</m> as a particle is moving from <m>(1,2)</m> to <m>(4,-3).</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				The direction vector is <m>\vec{v} = \langle 4-1, -3-2 \rangle = \langle 3, -5 \rangle</m>.
				The unit vector is <m>\vec{u} = \frac{\vec{v}}{|\vec{v}|} = \langle \frac{3}{\sqrt{34}}, -\frac{5}{\sqrt{34}} \rangle</m>.
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\nabla f \amp = \langle 2xy - \pi y \sin(\pi xy), x^2 - \pi x \sin(\pi xy) \rangle \\
					\nabla f(1,2) \amp = \langle 4 - 2\pi \sin(2\pi), 1 - \pi \sin(2\pi) \rangle = \langle 4, 1 \rangle \\
					D_{\vec{u}} f(1,2) \amp = \nabla f(1,2) \cdot \vec{u} = \langle 4, 1 \rangle \cdot \langle \frac{3}{\sqrt{34}}, -\frac{5}{\sqrt{34}} \rangle = \frac{12 - 5}{\sqrt{34}} = \frac{7}{\sqrt{34}}
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the function <me>f(x,y) = 2x^2 -8xy +y^4 -4y^3.</me>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find all critical point(s) of <m>f(x,y)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						f_x \amp = 4x - 8y = 0 \implies x = 2y \\
						f_y \amp = -8x + 4y^3 - 12y^2 = 0 \\
						\amp \implies -8(2y) + 4y^3 - 12y^2 = 0 \\
						\amp \implies 4y^3 - 12y^2 - 16y = 0 \\
						\amp \implies 4y(y^2 - 3y - 4) = 0 \implies 4y(y-4)(y+1) = 0
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					The <m>y</m>-coordinates are <m>y=0, 4, -1</m>. Using <m>x=2y</m>, the critical points are <m>(0,0), (8,4), (-2,-1)</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Classify each critical point you found in (a) as a local maximum, local minimum, or saddle point.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						f_{xx} \amp = 4, \quad f_{xy} = -8, \quad f_{yy} = 12y^2 - 24y \\
						D \amp = f_{xx}f_{yy} - (f_{xy})^2 = 4(12y^2 - 24y) - 64
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					<ul>
						<li>At <m>(0,0)</m>: <m>D = 4(0) - 64 = -64 \lt 0 \implies</m> saddle point.</li>
						<li>At <m>(8,4)</m>: <m>D = 4(12(16) - 24(4)) - 64 = 4(192 - 96) - 64 = 384 - 64 = 320 \gt 0</m>. Since <m>f_{xx} = 4 \gt 0 \implies</m> local minimum.</li>
						<li>At <m>(-2,-1)</m>: <m>D = 4(12(1) - 24(-1)) - 64 = 4(12 + 24) - 64 = 144 - 64 = 80 \gt 0</m>. Since <m>f_{xx} = 4 \gt 0 \implies</m> local minimum.</li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of the function <m>f(x,y)=
							y^2-4x^2</m> subject to the constraint <m>x^2+2y^2=4</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Let <m>g(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 = 4</m>.
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\nabla f \amp = \lambda \nabla g \\
					\langle -8x, 2y \rangle \amp = \lambda \langle 2x, 4y \rangle
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				This gives:
				<ol>
					<li><m>-8x = 2\lambda x \implies 2x(\lambda + 4) = 0 \implies x=0</m> or <m>\lambda = -4</m>.</li>
					<li><m>2y = 4\lambda y \implies 2y(1 - 2\lambda) = 0 \implies y=0</m> or <m>\lambda = 1/2</m>.</li>
				</ol>
				Case 1: <m>x=0</m>. From constraint, <m>2y^2 = 4 \implies y^2 = 2 \implies y = \pm \sqrt{2}</m>. Points: <m>(0, \pm\sqrt{2})</m>.
				Case 2: <m>\lambda = -4</m>. From (2), <m>2y = -16y \implies y=0</m>. From constraint, <m>x^2 = 4 \implies x = \pm 2</m>. Points: <m>(\pm 2, 0)</m>.
				Compare values:
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					f(0, \pm\sqrt{2}) \amp = (\pm\sqrt{2})^2 - 0 = 2 \\
					f(\pm 2, 0) \amp = 0 - 4(\pm 2)^2 = -16
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				The absolute maximum is 2, and the absolute minimum is -16.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Compute the following integrals.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\int_0^{4}\int_{\sqrt{y}}^{2} e^{x^3}\, dx\, dy</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Change order of integration: <m>0 \le y \le 4, \sqrt{y} \le x \le 2</m> becomes <m>0 \le x \le 2, 0 \le y \le x^2</m>.
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\int_0^2 \int_0^{x^2} e^{x^3} \, dy \, dx \amp = \int_0^2 x^2 e^{x^3} \, dx \\
						\amp = \left[ \frac{1}{3} e^{x^3} \right]_0^2 = \frac{1}{3}(e^8 - 1)
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Compute <m>\displaystyle\int_{-4}^{0}\int_{-\sqrt{16-x^2}}^{0} \cos(2x^2+2y^2) \, dy \, dx</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The region is the third quadrant of the disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 16</m>.
					In polar coordinates: <m>0 \le r \le 4, \pi \le \theta \le 3\pi/2</m>.
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\int_\pi^{3\pi/2} \int_0^4 \cos(2r^2) r \, dr \, d\theta \amp = \int_\pi^{3\pi/2} \left[ \frac{1}{4} \sin(2r^2) \right]_0^4 \, d\theta \\
						\amp = \int_\pi^{3\pi/2} \frac{1}{4} \sin(32) \, d\theta \\
						\amp = \frac{\pi}{8} \sin(32)
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Write down triple integrals that represent the volume of the following solids. You do <term>NOT</term> need to evaluate the integrals.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The solid bounded above by the cone <m>z = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2}</m>, below by the cone <m>z =
											-\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}</m>, and enclosed between the spheres <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1</m> and <m>x^2 + y^2 +
											z^2 = 7</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Using spherical coordinates: <m>1 \le \rho \le \sqrt{7}</m>, <m>0 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>, and <m>\pi/4 \le \phi \le 3\pi/4</m>.
					<me>V = \int_0^{2\pi} \int_{\pi/4}^{3\pi/4} \int_1^{\sqrt{7}} \rho^2 \sin \phi \, d\rho \, d\phi \, d\theta.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The solid in the first octant that lies within the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2 =4,</m> above the paraboloid <m>z = x^2+y^2 +1,</m> and below the plane <m>z = 9.</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Using cylindrical coordinates in the first octant: <m>0 \le \theta \le \pi/2</m>, <m>0 \le r \le 2</m>, and <m>r^2+1 \le z \le 9</m>.
					<me>V = \int_0^{\pi/2} \int_0^2 \int_{r^2+1}^9 r \, dz \, dr \, d\theta.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the solid in the first octant that is bounded by the coordinate planes and the plane <m>4x+2y+6z=12.</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write down an integral that represents the volume of the solid.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The plane equation is <m>4x + 2y + 6z = 12 \implies 2x + y + 3z = 6</m>.
					In the first octant: <m>0 \le x \le 3</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 6 - 2x</m>, <m>0 \le z \le \frac{6-2x-y}{3}</m>.
					<me>V = \int_0^3 \int_0^{6-2x} \int_0^{\frac{6-2x-y}{3}} dz \, dy \, dx.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Compute the volume of the solid by computing the triple integral found in part (a).
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						V \amp = \int_0^3 \int_0^{6-2x} \frac{6-2x-y}{3} \, dy \, dx \\
						\amp = \frac{1}{3} \int_0^3 \left[ (6-2x)y - \frac{y^2}{2} \right]_0^{6-2x} \, dx \\
						\amp = \frac{1}{3} \int_0^3 \left( (6-2x)^2 - \frac{(6-2x)^2}{2} \right) \, dx \\
						\amp = \frac{1}{3} \int_0^3 \frac{(6-2x)^2}{2} \, dx = \frac{1}{6} \int_0^3 (6-2x)^2 \, dx \\
						\amp = \frac{1}{6} \left[ -\frac{(6-2x)^3}{6} \right]_0^3 = \frac{1}{6} \left( 0 - (-\frac{216}{6}) \right) = \frac{1}{6}(36) = 6.
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</worksheet>
